AWS IAM Foundations Project
Secure AWS Starter Account (Least Privilege + MFA + Role Switching)
📌 Project Goal

Design and implement a secure AWS IAM foundation that follows security best practices:

Never use the root account for daily work

Enforce MFA for IAM users

Implement least privilege

Use role assumption instead of permanent elevated access

Create a break-glass emergency administrator

This project simulates how a real organization should secure a new AWS account from day one.

🏗 Architecture Overview

Identities Created

Root Account (MFA enabled)

breakglass-admin (emergency administrator)

blake-daily (standard daily user)

Admins group

Users group

ReadOnlyRole

Security Controls Implemented

MFA enforced

Least privilege model

Role-based access control (RBAC)

Deny access if MFA not present

Temporary privilege elevation via STS AssumeRole

🔐 Step 1 – Root Account Hardening
Action Taken

Enabled MFA on the AWS root account.

Why This Matters

The root account has unrestricted access to everything in AWS.
If compromised, the entire account is compromised.

Security Best Practice

Enable MFA immediately.

Never use root for daily operations.

👥 Step 2 – Admin Group Creation
Action Taken

Created a group:

Admins


Attached AWS Managed Policy:

AdministratorAccess

Why This Matters

Permissions should be attached to groups, not directly to users.
This improves scalability and reduces configuration errors.

🚨 Step 3 – Break-Glass Admin User
Action Taken

Created user:

breakglass-admin


Console access enabled

Added to Admins group

MFA assigned

Purpose

Emergency access only.

Real-World Use Case

If the daily user is locked out or IAM policies break access, this account restores control.

👤 Step 4 – Daily User Creation
Action Taken

Created user:

blake-daily


Console access enabled

NOT added to Admin group

MFA assigned

Security Principle Applied

Least Privilege.

Daily work should never use full administrator access.

🔒 Step 5 – Enforcing MFA (ForceMFA Policy)
Custom Policy Created

Policy Name:

ForceMFA

Policy JSON
{
"Version":"2012-10-17",
"Statement":[
{
"Sid":"AllowViewAccountInfo",
"Effect":"Allow",
"Action":[
"iam:GetAccountPasswordPolicy",
"iam:ListVirtualMFADevices",
"iam:ListUsers",
"iam:ListMFADevices"
],
"Resource":"*"
},
{
"Sid":"AllowManageOwnPasswords",
"Effect":"Allow",
"Action":[
"iam:ChangePassword",
"iam:GetUser"
],
"Resource":"*"
},
{
"Sid":"AllowManageOwnMFA",
"Effect":"Allow",
"Action":[
"iam:CreateVirtualMFADevice",
"iam:DeleteVirtualMFADevice",
"iam:EnableMFADevice",
"iam:DeactivateMFADevice",
"iam:ResyncMFADevice",
"iam:ListMFADevices"
],
"Resource":"*"
},
{
"Sid":"DenyAllExceptListedIfNoMFA",
"Effect":"Deny",
"NotAction":[
"iam:CreateVirtualMFADevice",
"iam:EnableMFADevice",
"iam:GetUser",
"iam:ListMFADevices",
"iam:ListVirtualMFADevices",
"iam:ChangePassword",
"sts:GetSessionToken"
],
"Resource":"*",
"Condition":{
"BoolIfExists":{
"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent":"false"
}
}
}
]
}

Applied To

Created group:

Users


Attached ForceMFA policy and added:

blake-daily

Outcome

If a user logs in without MFA:

Most AWS actions are denied.

If MFA is present:

Access behaves normally.

Real-World Value

Prevents stolen passwords from being enough to compromise an account.

🔄 Step 6 – ReadOnly Role + Role Switching
Role Created
ReadOnlyRole


Trusted Entity:

This AWS account

Permission Policy Attached:

ReadOnlyAccess

Inline Policy for Daily User

Attached to blake-daily:

{
"Version":"2012-10-17",
"Statement":[
{
"Effect":"Allow",
"Action":"sts:AssumeRole",
"Resource":"arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:role/ReadOnlyRole"
}
]
}


(Replace <ACCOUNT_ID> with actual account ID.)

Tested Role Switching

Logged in as:

blake-daily


Used:
Top-right profile → Switch Role → Enter Account ID + ReadOnlyRole

Successfully assumed role and gained temporary read-only permissions.

